            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <mainWalls>

The main exterior walls and basement pony walls (excluding garage and concrete walls) accounts for 76% of the total exterior wall area, and it appears to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation+2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to these walls.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to these walls, you could potentially save $ 500 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,900 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: Our available rebate funding for this year has currently been spoken for. Any new applications will be placed on a waiting list. We can't guarantee that you will receive a rebate.</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (12 total between the basement and main floor) are triple pane no low e vinyl windows. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 220 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 182 litre external storage buffer tank. The boiler has a 5" flue vent &amp; there is a 6" open combustion air supply into the crawlspace. The online specs for that boiler indicate it had 82.6% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE).



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 5" chimney or the 6" combustion air supply pipe.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 50 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: Our available rebate funding for this year has currently been spoken for. Any new applications will be placed on a waiting list. We can't guarantee that you will receive a rebate.

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Isolated heat generated by pellet stove - There are some ways you can move that isolated heat from basement to the main floor, I can think of two ways that can be done:

Passively: By creating few grate size openings between main floor and basement. This way hot air can circulate through the main floor living room and partially to the surrounding areas.

Mechanically: Currently your fan coil appears to only draw air from upstairs, and redistribute it back upstairs. An option may be to have a contractor connect an additional ?????????intake????????? vent located in the basement living room area, so that it also pulls air from downstairs. By using the fan coil to recirculate the air around the home, and having the ability to draw the warm pellet stove air from downstairs, and evenly distribute the warm air upstairs.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:

** All bathroom exhaust fans. It looks like the exhaust fan damper is not sealing well and could be replaced with the new ENERGY STAR* exhaust fan with butterfly damper.

** The basement patio door is not sealing properly due to a broken latch. Installing a new latch will reduce some of the air leakage from that door.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

We noticed that your house has incandescent and halogen lights for the most part. I would recommend changing those to LED lights.

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a 300 CFM range hood   

        - three bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Dec.1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: abdul.mohammed@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
